I was sitting in my bedroom wearing my  comfiest sweatpants slamming on my  keyboard I was working on this talk  searching for the words for an  inspirational hopefully not too preachy  call for all of us to work on bridging  the divides in American culture and  politics I was writing something like  each and every one of us should be  concerned about the future of democracy  when something had caught my eye it was  a framed picture on my dresser of my  college friends of me at our graduation  this photo contains two Meghan's  - Lawrence - Kate's and my best friend  Andrea all of us grew up on the u.s.  coast after college each of us moved  back to the u.s. coast almost all of us  with a few exceptions have gone to  graduate school we're living in the Bay  Area Los Angeles Boston and New York  working in business medicine and law and  staying in touch with G with each other  by emailing around criticisms of Donald  Trump looking at this picture it became  so clear to me things like this are  where the National disconnects start we  as humans seek out others who are like  us foreclose social connection  psychologists call this Hamas aliy  meaning birds of a feather flock  together  think of your closest friends are they  similar to you and each other in race  and age did you go to the same kind of  school or do you make roughly the same  amount of money do you hold similar  views on religion and politics if so  this is completely natural we do it for  good reason  groups whose members share the same  backgrounds and vocabularies and  purposes conform quickly and provide  their members with substantial support  for minority groups bonding together  like this can be particularly valuable  because it affirms a sense of identity  and increases collective resources in  the face of oppression so some of this  is good but our society has overshot the  mark we live today with levels of  disconnectedness and division that are  not the result only a proverbial birds  of a feather flocking together but also  of insidious institution level forces  that have separated us to the point  where different Americans often see each  other as different species for one  there's a news media which allows us to  hear only one side of a story I know I  indulge this opportunity  checking the New York Times home page  multiple times per day but never  watching Fox News also we're divided by  where we live segregated along both  racial and economic lines this story is  told by the Jefferson counties  Jefferson County Mississippi is 86  percent black Jefferson County Missouri  is 95 percent white Stanford Business  School graduates beeline to New York and  San Francisco after graduation whereas  the average American lives within 18  miles of his or her mom and then there's  income inequality the result of macro  trends like globalization and automation  which have put many out of work while  the highly educated have gotten richer  I'll be honest here that this problem  has taken on a new sense of importance  and personal urgency for me after this  year's presidential election but why did  it take a presidential election for me  to get focused on such an impactful  issue this isn't just birds of a feather  flocking together this is a problem of  exposure Americans today are divided  economically culturally politically and  many of us have just passively fallen  into this pattern the forces that divide  us are so powerful and so pervasive that  we've come to see disunity as acceptable  because it's inevitable  it is not inevitable it's not inevitable  because each of us has the ability not  only to bond together with those who are  like us but also to bridge with those  who are not we can resist the forces  that pull us apart by forming personal  relationships with people who are  different from us and if we're to live  up to our ideals of democracy and  diversity we must the case for bridging  is strong everyone from the founding  fathers to today's political scientists  have argued that Americans need to form  personal relationships across  demographic lines in order to make  democracy and a heterogeneous country  work but you don't have to care about  democracy in a heterogeneous country  bridging is simply good for us as people  it rounds out our perspective and  teaches a thing that we otherwise  wouldn't learn it makes us better  business people who really know our  customers and better policymakers who  really know our constituents I believe  many of us already have the inclination  to put ourselves in someone else's shoes  but without shared experience do we  really have the ability there are three  simple ways that we can bridge first  recognize the diversity within our own  groups  remember my college friends my best  friend Andrea is a teacher in inner city  charter schools I used to be a banker so  while our friendship represents bonding  in many ways it's actually bridging  along this dimension of profession I  have learned so much from Andrea hearing  about her students and their lives  befriending her co-workers who are  career educators visiting her schools in  downtown Newark New Jersey and Hunters  Point San Francisco these experiences  have given me an empathy for the  challenges that low-income students in  urban environments face and this issue  is now extremely important to me we all  have our Andrea's who can show us  something outside of our usual routine  second let's change the way we have  conversations we miss out on so many  opportunity  needs to bridge because we try to argue  and convince rather than listen and  understand some of us here may be guilty  of this in the last political argument  that we had we my mom who's here today  and appears to be crying has another way  she is a world class chatter and we joke  in our family that if you leave her  alone in a grocery store line for even a  few minutes she'll know the life story  of the person standing next to her and  the cashier by the time you get back  this daily practice prepares her well  for more meaningful bridging  relationships  she was an effective advocate for  lessons in the foster care system and is  a fantastic psychologist for the  mentally ill individual because she  listens and empathizes and keeps an open  mind  my mom's interpersonal relationships  brought in her exposure giving her a  well tested yet ever-evolving view on  the world I aspire to that third  recognize the things in yourself that  might be different someone else and find  ways to share it my Jewish friends are  great at this they've invited me to  Shabbat dinners and Yom Kippur break  fast and these have been experiences to  share in their cultures that I've  treasured if this sounds a little  awkward or uncomfortable to you take  heart in the fact that Samantha Power's  the former US Representative to the  United Nations befriended her Russian  counterpart and frequent opponent by  taking him to theater performances in  Central Park or Knicks games in Madison  Square Garden when he was in New York  for UN meetings she can manage to do  this we should be excited to welcome  others into our world these steps may  seem small that's the point our society  can be transformed through individual  relationships these relationships are  ends in and of themselves as well as  means to more informed and compassionate  policymaking and busy  practices the sooner we start bridging  the sooner this becomes a habit even a  way of life imagine the possibilities  when each of us applies our creativity  and ingenuity towards understanding  people who look and think and live  differently from us with more of us  building bridges the prospects for a  more United future are great thank you  [Applause]  [Music]
 

